g rather than move into the pre-merge battery"), so it contradicts nothing: the guard **SKIPs on an `ACTIVE` lock line**, so it is a no-op mid-fire and bites only after the handoff commit, which is when the desk exists. ✅ `gate-caller-audit` re-run after the change: **PASS** (reachability is computed from ROOTS and `test:ledger-guards` is deliberately unrooted, so the grandfather entry stays correct and stays needed). 🩹 **THE INHERITED DESK IS REPAIRED IN THIS FIRE'S OWN HANDOFF, not merely reported:** all **21** items are carried forward with **one 🔺 each**, restoring the format the parser reads. The true set is not my reconstruction — it is `desk-state-audit --status` run against **s1664's** archived line-1, which returns `CLOSED=0 · OPEN=0 · BOTH=0 · OPEN-DESK-ONLY=21 · UNRECORDED=0`, i.e. **nothing among the 21 has silently closed and none is a ghost.** ⓘ **No new mechanism is proposed for the `·`-run encoding itself** — the count refusal already catches it (`declared 21` vs `keyed 1`), and it went unseen only because the guard holding that check was never run. **Wiring the existing guard is the whole cure.**
⏸️ **F-1666-1 (s1666 2026-08-11, MEASURED ON A DRY BOARD — THE DISPATCH-HOLD EVERY FIRE SINCE s1664 HAS INHERITED IS CORRECT, BUT IT WAS KEYED TO A CLOCK THAT UNDER-STATES IT, AND THE ONE READY MASTER IS NOW PROVEN DISPATCH-READY SO THE NEXT FIRE SPENDS NO BUDGET RE-DERIVING IT. NOT AN OWNER QUESTION.)** 📋 **THE BOARD, VERIFIED NOT INHERITED:** six queues EMPTY · no undrained done-move (every `tasks/done/` head entry carries `drained-`/`stopped-`) · no `tasks/CODEX-WALL` · assayer `pending/` EMPTY · `authorable-candidates` → **14 planned, 14 priced, 0 fire-authorable** (owner-gated 7 · attended-owed 5 · prose-only 1 · needs-spec 1). **So there was nothing to drain and nothing lawful to author — the entire question this fire faced was whether to DISPATCH the one banked master.** ✅ **DISPATCH PRECONDITIONS PROVEN, WHICH IS THIS FIRE'S DELIVERABLE:** `tasks/f1662-2-audit-exemption-count.md` (LANE-B, authored s1663) carries five pre-flight keys, and **all five return exactly 1 on main right now** — re-measured, not inherited from the authoring fire: `leaving five cited exemptions` · `ten of those fifteen passed below and were admitted` · `### Cited exemptions` (all `scripts/same-game-audit.mjs`) · `'e2-incline': {` (`src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts`) · `F-1662-2 MASTER AUTHORED s1663` (`tasks/BACKLOG.md`). **`main == origin/main == 65d37381d`**, so the master's unconditional STEP-1 refresh onto `origin/main` will carry every one of them into `lane/b` (`ahead=0`, USABLE, 37 behind). The F-1425-2 paraphrase trap and the F-1424-3 one-commit-behind trap are both **cleared in advance**. ⚠️ **BUT THE HOLD ITSELF IS REAL AND IS NOW GROUNDED TWICE OVER, WHERE s1664/s1665 GROUNDED IT ONCE.** (1) **f1643-2's own gate**, read at source rather than transcribed: *"author this only when all four lanes are idle and no drain is in flight, and have it state the concurrent load it ran under."* (2) **THE OWNER'S THROTTLE LAW OF THIS SAME DAY**, which is the stronger and previously-uncited half — verbatim *"can we limit the amount of threads it uses? I was just surprised it took so long. I woke up in the middle of the night to it."* → the run was re-queued with `--workers=3` + `nice -n 19` baked in. **A second runner at normal priority would starve a deliberately-niced run and re-create exactly the saturation the owner ruled against hours earlier.** 🎯 **THE CORRECTION, and it is the reusable half: THE HOLD WAS KEYED TO A CLOCK, AND THE CLOCK UNDER-STATES THE WINDOW.** s1665 handed off *"should land ~19:35"*. That estimate is wrong twice. **(a) Arithmetically** — re-measured from the live log, 2,254@18:44 → 2,352@19:00 → 2,442@19:10, i.e. ~9/min with 362 executions left, so the PARALLEL arm alone lands ≈19:50. **(b) Structurally, which is the part no better arithmetic fixes** — the parallel arm is not the end of the load-sensitive window. **Scope 5 requires re-running every failing spec FILE serially (`--workers=1`) after the full run, and that serial arm IS the discriminator between load-attributable and real reds** (451 failures observed at last read). A test that is load-attributable but *also* fails serially because a fire added load during the re-run is classified **REAL** and enters the inventory as a fresh excused red — which F-1643-2's own pricing calls *"strictly worse than the 13-day-old one it replaced."* **A fire that reads "~19:35", waits until 19:40 and dispatches would do the right thing at the wrong moment and silently poison the snapshot.** ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING FOR THE NEXT FIRES — KEY THE HOLD ON THE INSTRUMENT, NOT THE CLOCK: dispatch `f1662-2` when `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs lane-a` stops saying BUSY, and not before.** ✓ **VERIFIED IN THE CODE, not assumed:** `lane-usable.mjs:292` derives BUSY from `tasks/running/<slot>.pid` (live: `tasks/running/lane-a.pid` = 61900) via `isSlotBusy`, which fails safe to BUSY on anything it cannot establish (`:279–:287`). The runner holds that pidfile for the **whole** task, scope 5 included —