ard-guard.mjs` (s1533), `desk-state-audit.mjs` (s1566) and `review-evidence-audit.mjs` (s1576); **four in 125 fires is no longer an anecdote, and the run-it-after-the-commit step is the only thing that has ever caught it.** 💡 **THE LESSON, and it generalises past art: when a defect is cured in an INSTRUMENT, ask the same question of every sibling that acts on its verdict. The audit was fixed three times by three careful fires, and not one of them asked whether the thing the audit TELLS YOU TO RUN had kept up.** A remedy text is a citation like any other, and citations rot — [[verify-the-target-surface-of-a-prescribed-cure]].
🟢 **F-1658-2 (s1658 2026-08-11, CURED THE SAME FIRE — THE MECHANICAL ANSWER TO F-1640-1'S OPEN MYSTERY: THE BOARD ITSELF PRINTED ZERO, EVERY TEN MINUTES, FOR ~106 FIRES).** F-1640-1 records that the Retention Law's largest hole *"fell off the owner's desk ~106 fires ago and no fire noticed"*, and attributes the silence to the ART-SLOT LAW keying its audit duty on TOUCHING the art slot — *"which no rule obliged me to run"*. **That is only half the cause, and the other half is not a policy gap but a broken instrument.** ✓ **MEASURED LIVE, from the real script, before any edit: `bash scripts/health-watch.sh status` printed `art staged, in NO commit: 0 file(s)` at the same instant the audit reported `AT RISK 582 files / 527.89 MB`.** 🔑 **`scripts/health-watch.sh:41 art_untracked()` fed BOTH the dashboard line (`:78`) and the edge-triggered alert (`:139`) — the one that prints the salvage remedy — and it carried BOTH defects the audit had already been fixed for:** it read only `worktrees/art/assets/raw`, NON-recursively (**F-1120-2**, while 100% of the live exposure is nested under `motion-pilot/`), and it pre-filtered by NAME through `comm -23` against `ls-tree main -- assets/raw/` (**F-1054-1**), dropping regenerated bytes before its otherwise-correct blob check could ever see them. ⚠️ **THE ALERT COULD NEVER HAVE FIRED, BY CONSTRUCTION — AND THAT IS WORSE THAN A MISSING ALARM.** It is deliberately EDGE-triggered on the count (`:138`, and rightly so: a level-triggered alert every 10 minutes would train everyone to ignore it). **A count that never leaves `0` has no edges.** So the factory did not merely fail to look — **it looked, continuously, and was told there was nothing there.** Three fires fixed the audit and none asked what the DASHBOARD was reading. ✅ **CURED `4ddd9d35e`:** enumerated + recursive + classified by BLOB, batched through two git plumbing processes instead of one per file (~10 s over 983 files, comfortable inside a 10-minute loop). **The dashboard no