🔧 **F-1616-2 (s1616 2026-08-10, CONFIRMED BY MANUFACTURE AT THE f1614-1 DRAIN — THE NEW SPEC REWRITES A *TRACKED* `reviews/*.md` ON EVERY RUN, WHICH IS A HARD STOP IN BOTH PRE-FLIGHT TEMPLATES, AND IT HIDES WHENEVER THE LAST PROJECT AGREES WITH THE COMMITTED BYTES.)** ⚙️ **WHAT IT IS.** `e2e/advance-stream-walkthrough.spec.ts` writes `reviews/advance-stream-walkthrough.md` — a **tracked** file — every time the test runs. Both pre-flight templates in `.claude/skills/author-task/SKILL.md` (LANE **and** MAIN) end with the same sentence: *"What still STOPs: modified tracked `src/**`, `scripts/**`, `e2e/**`, `tasks/**`, `specs/**`, `reviews/*.md`."* The **FACTORY-CHURN (F-1407-1)** and **EVIDENCE-ARTIFACT (F-1266-1)** exceptions cover `artifacts/**`, `reviews/shots-*` and `.png` — **`reviews/*.md` is deliberately excluded from both.** ✅ **MANUFACTURED, NOT ARGUED (the s1299/s1300 standard — a passing check is not evidence about the failing path):** a desktop-only run of the spec on the merged tree, then `git status --short` → `` M artifacts/advance-stream-walkthrough.md`` and `` M reviews/advance-stream-walkthrough.md``. **That is the exact predicate `f1406-1` died on — 54,875 tokens for zero edits.** ⚠️ **THE NASTY PART IS WHY IT DID NOT SHOW UP IN THE RUNNER’S OWN GREEN RUN:** playwright runs projects in declared order, **mobile runs last**, and mobile’s numbers happened to reproduce the committed file byte-for-byte — the desktop arm had already written `town-return 4|6` over it and been overwritten back. **A trap that fires only when the last project disagrees is worse than one that always fires, because the board reads clean until the day it does not.** 📐 **TWO AGGRAVATIONS, BOTH STRUCTURAL.** (1) ✓ Verified across every `writeFile(` call site in `e2e/`: every other spec writes solely to `artifacts/**` or `reviews/shots-*` — **this is the only spec in the repo writing a tracked `reviews/*.md`**, so the convention exists and this is its sole exception. (2) Both projects write **the same path**, so at the 6 workers a lane shell uses (`playwright.config.ts:50`, `workers: isFireShell ? 1 : undefined`) the two project instances race on one file. ➡️ **CURE: write only `artifacts/`, under a per-project filename; keep the committed `reviews/advance-stream-walkthrough.md` as the static owner-facing record it already is.** The owner deliverable is *a record of a measurement*, not a file that must silently re-derive itself under every unrelated suite run. ✅ **AUTHORED THIS FIRE as `f1616-1` (lane-c).** **GATE: none owed to the owner.**
🔧 **F-1616-3 (s1616 2026-08-10, MEASURED — THE WARM COLUMN MEASURES REQUEST *ORDERING*, NOT CACHE WARMTH, AND THE TABLE’S OWN NUMBERS ARE THE PROOF. THE INTERESTING HALF IS DELIBERATELY LEFT UNVERIFIED.)** ⚙️ Both columns of the walkthrough table are computed from `door.demands`, which by construction holds only requests **without** the prefetch header. So a URL is counted WARM **iff it was prefetched to completion and then demand-requested again anyway.** ✓ **This is not a reading of the code — it is visible in the numbers: `WARM > 0` is only possible when a completed prefetch failed to prevent a second request.** Town shows 2 such URLs; contract1 shows 7. Meanwhile `src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts:191-193` prefetches with `cache: 'force-cache'`, so the prefetch’s entire value proposition **is** a populated HTTP cache — and in this harness that cache is demonstrably not being reused. ➡️ **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE DELIVERABLE (non-blocking, and it does not impeach the table):** it faithfully answers *"was this door’s fetch preceded by a completed prefetch?"* and **not** the question the owner’s phrasing asks, *"what was already warm at each door?"* — an asset genuinely served warm from cache fires no request and lands in **neither** column, so the table understates warmth and overstates coldness by an unknown amount. ⚠️ **UNVERIFIED, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS THE PART WORTH SOMEONE’S TIME:** whether the re-request is an artifact of `page.route('**/*.glb')` interception bypassing the browser cache, **or a real property of play.** ⭐ **If it is real, the advance stream downloads its assets twice in normal play** — a genuine performance defect and a much larger finding than this drain. **Distinguishing them costs one probe: walk the same doors with NO `page.route` at all and count requests per URL.** 🚫 **Not attempted here on purpose** — out of the drain’s scope, and it is a measurement, not a guess to be recorded as fact. **GATE: none owed to the owner; any fire may take the probe (one per fire).** ➡️ **TAKEN s1619 — authored and queued as `f1619-1` to lane-c (`tasks/lane-f1619-1-advance-stream-cache-reuse.md`, row below). The one-per-fire allowance is SPENT for s1619.** ✅ **ANSWERED s1620, merge `beb926087` — HALF OF IT. The re-request is NOT a `page.route` artifact: it reproduces with zero interception, identically, four runs over.** 🚫 **BUT THE OTHER HALF OF THIS FINDING'S OWN QUESTION IS STILL OPEN, AND THE PROBE COULD NOT HAVE CLOSED IT — see F-1620-2.** This line asked *artifact, or a real property of play?* and treated those as exhaustive; they are not, because **the harness has a second cache confound the probe inherited rather than removed.** The walk runs against `npm run dev`, and Vite serves GLBs with `Cache-Control: no-cache`, under which the browser MUST revalidate and a true cache hit **cannot occur at all** — so `CACHE-HIT 0` was determined by the server before the prefetch was ever consulted. **Removing `page.route` removed the confound this line named and left the one it did not.** 💡 *The reusable half: an A/B that eliminates the suspect you thought of still inherits every suspect you didn't — and a `0` in the column you were watching is the most persuasive possible way for that to hide.* ➡️ **F-1616-3 is now CLOSED as asked and SUCCEEDED BY F-1620-2, which carries the live question: does this happen against production headers?**
