🟢 **F-1601-1** (s1601 2026-08-09, MEASURED AND PARTLY DISCHARGED THIS FIRE — A THIRD OF THE OWNER'S DESK IS NOT WAITING ON THE OWNER, AND THAT IS WHY THE BOARD READ DRY FOR FIVE CONSECUTIVE FIRES). Found by auditing the desk I inherited instead of carrying it. ⚠️ **THE HEADER'S OWN WORDS ARE FALSE FOR A THIRD OF WHAT IT COUNTS.** The handoff declares `🔺 OWNER'S DESK — 31 awaiting a word`. ✓ **MEASURED by reading the GATE clause of all 28 desk findings plus the 3 blocked slugs, one row at a time — not grepped, because the gate is free prose and a grep reports its hits and never its misses.** The 31 split three ways: **19 genuinely turn on a word** (12 direct owner words, 2 dependent on another finding's owner ruling — F-1532-2 on F-1167-1, F-1528-3 on F-1499-2 —, 2 attended-or-owner, 3 `blocked` slugs all `owner-fork`); **9 name a WORK ACT no owner can supply** — a measurement, a doc line, a code edit, a lane run; and **2 state in their own text that nothing is owed at all**: F-1589-3 (*"GATE: none owed; re-measure if it reddens in isolation"*) and F-1588-1 (*"GATE: none — this is a confirming measurement, not a defect. Non-blocking, no cure owed"*). 🔑 **THOSE LAST TWO CAN NEVER LEAVE THE DESK.** They are informational records parked in a queue whose only exit is an owner word they explicitly do not want. This is **F-1383-1's shape one level up**: that finding split `status:"blocked"` into `owner-fork` vs `gate-side` precisely because an overloaded refusal parks a fire-liftable hold in front of the owner forever — the desk overloads the same way, and has no `blockClass` to save it. ⭐ **THE EXPENSIVE HALF IS THE 9, AND IT IS THE DRY BOARD SEEN FROM THE OTHER SIDE.** s1597–s1600 each declared **PIPELINE-DRY ×4 (needs spec/owner)** and authored nothing, while these sat on the desk labelled owner debt: **F-1587-2** (a warming step or a named-cause timeout) · **F-1591-1** (the Arm 0 clamp table) · **F-1589-2** (a re-snapshot or a staleness warning) · **F-1588-2** (merge two refusal paths, or document the ordering) · **F-1587-1** (re-measure per project) · **F-1507-1** (show both shells on one node version) · **F-1510-1** (two named tests green at `--workers=1`) · **F-1536-2** (bucket the 66 `save/*` refs) · **F-DOOR-6** (a door-doc slice lands (a)+(b); only (c) is owner-optional). **Four of those nine say inside their own row that a fire or a doc line may take them** — F-DOOR-5 and F-E2S-3 likewise offer a `§7.4` arm while their forks stay owner-gated. A dry board and a 31-item desk are not two facts; they are one fact filed under the wrong heading. ✅ **PROVED BY TAKING ONE, NOT BY ARGUING IT: F-1587-1 IS CURED THIS FIRE** (see its row) — measured, mechanised, guarded, committed, **no owner word involved**, in a single fire that began by being told the board was dry. That is the claim's control: if these were really owner debt, that could not have happened. 📐 **WHAT I AM NOT CLAIMING:** I did not re-price the 19, and the genuine owner forks are untouched and still owed — the desk is not "mostly noise", it is **~61% real and mis-sized by ~39%**. Nor is the count itself wrong: 31 items ARE carried, and `desk-state-audit --status` correctly returns `CLOSED=0 · OPEN=4 · OPEN-DESK-ONLY=27 · UNRECORDED=0` — **nothing carried should have been dropped.** The defect is not the arithmetic, it is the WORD "awaiting", which asserts a routing that a third of the items contradict in their own closing sentence. ➡️ **THE CHEAP CURE, RECOMMENDED AND DELIBERATELY NOT BUILT THIS FIRE:** give `scripts/desk-state-audit.mjs` a `gateClass` column — `owner-word` / `work-act` / `none-owed` — failing safe to `owner-word` on anything unrecognised, exactly as `blockClass` does, since the dangerous direction is routing a real fork to a fire. ⛔ **I did not write it, on purpose:** classifying free prose by heuristic at the tail of a fire is how a fragile classifier gets born, and the 9 are listed **by name above**, so the next dry-board fire needs no mechanism to act — it needs this row. **GATE: none for the 9 (any fire may take them, one per fire); an attended nod for the `gateClass` column; the 19 stay owner-gated and unchanged. Reverse the whole row with one word — nothing was deleted, F-1589-3 and F-1588-1 keep their BACKLOG rows and only leave the DESK.** 📌 **CONVENTION, ADDED s1604 PER F-1603-1 — WHEN YOU TAKE ONE OF THE 9, FLIP THE ROW HEAD IN THE SAME COMMIT AS THE CURE: A TAIL-APPENDED `CURED` IS NOT A CLOSURE.** The closure detectors (`subjectLedClosure`, and `findings-state-guard`'s `scan` beneath it) read the row HEAD, not its body, while the house cure convention appends `✅ **CURED sNNNN` to the TAIL of a long row whose head keeps its open marker — so the cure is invisible to the detector. **Three consecutive work-act cures were invisible this way: s1601 (F-1587-1), s1602 (F-1589-2), s1603 (F-1588-2)** — but ⚖️ **MEASURED s1604 BY REPLAYING `desk-carryforward-guard` AGAINST ALL THREE FIRES' OWN HANDOFFS, ONLY ONE ACTUALLY TRIPPED IT, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE USEFUL PART.** s1602: `dropped:[F-1589-2] · silent:[F-1589-2]`, **rc=1 — a handoff its own last-act battery would have REFUSED**; s1601: dropped 3, `silent:[]`, saved because its line-1 ACKNOWLEDGED each drop in prose; s1603: `silent:[]`, saved because it flipped its own head. ➡️ **So there are two ways to stay green and only one is durable:** an acknowledgement clause saves the FIRE that writes it and nothing after, while a flipped head makes the closure readable to every reader forever. Write the head flip; the acknowledgement is not a substitute. ✓ **DISCHARGED s1604 for all three** — F-1588-2 flipped by s1603, and F-1587-1 (`22ff19c1d`) + F-1589-2 (`c889c0539`) flipped this fire after confirming each cure landed **in the code, not the prose**: the live instrument prints both behaviours (`! CORRECTION … OUTRANKS THE SNAPSHOT BELOW` above the stale bucket; `! STALE SNAPSHOT — 12 days old, threshold 7` per-outcome between verdict and body), `red-inventory-lookup.test.mjs` 18/18, and both hashes carry the claimed files. All three now read `closed` at their own line. ⚠️ **Cite F-1603-1 in the flipped head if you like, but know why it is safe:** classification is subject-FIRST, so an id merely cited after the row's own id carries no state — **verified as a control this fire, F-1603-1 stayed OPEN across both flips.**

🟢 **F-1600-1** (s1600 2026-08-09, MEASURED AND CURED THIS FIRE, DOC-ONLY — THE GAZETTE SWEEP’S HASH CHECK IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THE SECOND OWNER-FACING SINK, SO 44.7% OF ITS “ABSENT” VERDICTS ARE ITEMS THE OWNER HAS ALREADY BEEN SHOWN). Found while discharging s1599’s deliberately-deferred (B). ⚠️ **THE SENTENCE THIS REFUTES IS A PRIOR FINDING’S EXONERATION.** F-1593-1 audited this very sweep, split it into halves, and wrote *“the hash half is a `grep` and cannot go wrong; the ‘real-change’ half needs the merge’s file list”* — then spent its entire investigation on the second half. **The hash half went wrong in two independent ways, and that exoneration is why nobody looked.** 🔑 **THE MECHANISM:** the news pipeline has TWO owner-facing sinks — GAZETTE items in `marketing/outbox/gazette-queue.md` and the daily TK-01 ticker digests sitting beside it — and the duty’s prescribed check greps ONE file for the FULL 40-char hash. 📐 **The two sinks cite hashes in DISJOINT conventions, which makes the blindness categorical rather than a sampling artifact — ✓ VERIFIED by counting every hash-shaped token in the live outbox: ticker digests carry 618 short hashes and ZERO full-40 ones; `gazette-queue.md` carries 86 full and 105 short.** A full-40 grep therefore **cannot** match a ticker digest in principle, however many fires run it. 📊 **MEASURED (`scripts/tmp-s1600-outbox-sinks.mjs`, window 2026-08-05→s1600): 1065 first-parent commits, 81 touching player paths (`src/` `functions/` `public/` `index.html` `e2e/`), of which the narrow check calls 47 ABSENT — but 20 are headlined in a ticker digest and 1 sits in the gazette under its short hash. So 21 of 47 are FALSE POSITIVES (44.7%), and only 26 are genuinely unreported.** ⚠️ **My denominator is NOT s1599’s, and I do not claim to have re-priced its exact figures:** s1599 filtered on commit-message prefix (53 shaped / 37 absent); I filtered on touched paths (81 / 47). **The transferable result is the RATE, not the count.** ⓘ **THE DAMAGE IS AN ITEM THAT CAN NEVER BE DISCHARGED, NOT WASTED GREPS:** `3b2b33c29` (“the county’s first standing — V4 Pro secures the-claim via the public door”) was left by s1599 as an UNJUDGED gazette candidate and re-judged by me — when it had been headlined to the owner in `ticker-digest-2026-08-06.md:27–29` on 2026-08-07, **carrying its own OWNER CHOICE note at `:58`**, since before either fire existed. A check that reports a discharged duty as owed reports it forever, and every fire pays the judgement cost again; this one was on its third pass. ✅ **VERDICT ON THAT CANDIDATE, so it stops circulating: REPORTED, NOT OWED — no gazette item, and it was never a real-change merge anyway** (its diff is `logs/seed-ladder/`, two run transcripts, `scripts/seed-ladder.mjs` +5/-1, `tasks/BACKLOG.md` +1 — no `src/`, no `functions/`). 💡 **THIS REPRICES ANY ABSENCE COUNT TAKEN THE NARROW WAY, INCLUDING F-1599-1’S** — if ~45% of such a set is already published, the backlog it implies is smaller than it reads, and a cure sized against the raw figure is over-sized. ⚖️ **BUT IT DOES NOT REFUTE F-1599-1, AND I CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED:** its instance `a97ff79c7` appears in the outbox today **only because s1599 backfilled it by hand** (`0760e05da`); re-greped at both hash lengths, **no ticker digest ever carried it**, so F-1599-1 was a TRUE miss and not an artifact of this defect. My finding changes the SIZE of that problem, not its existence. ✅ **CURED IN `scripts/fire.md` GZ-01 THIS FIRE:** the check is now “grep the 8-char SHORT hash across the whole of `marketing/outbox/`”. Doc-only, no code, no new guard — the duty’s own standing prohibition on mechanising this as a red guard is untouched and still right. **GATE: none — closed on landing. Reverse with one word if the owner prefers the narrow check.** ✅ **SWEEP DISCHARGED THIS FIRE WITH THE CORRECTED CHECK, WHICH IS HOW THE REST OF THIS ROW WAS LEARNED.** Of the 47 the narrow check called absent, **4** touch real player source once `e2e/` is dropped from the filter (a test is not a player-facing surface), and **3 of those 4 are genuine misses** — backfilled as one ROUNDUP per the ≤3/week rule: `c8807b4d` (THE FRONT DESK panel beside the county boards — `renderFrontDesk()` + 99 lines of `reader.css`), `7e5fae14` (the e2-trestle terrain rebuild, `Terrain3dClaimPilot.ts` +243 with new atlas/blend/glb), `d9cf6242` (a works with power lines but no connection to make would pin `objectiveAllowsSecure` false forever, so beating the Baron **won the contract and silently failed to secure it**). The 4th, `1a2871fd`, is factory hygiene (composite-key delimiters re-encoded so `grep` works in Claude shells) and is CORRECTLY absent. 🔎 **A THIRD BLINDNESS, found only by discharging the duty rather than measuring it — AND IT IS Mistake #16 SITTING INSIDE THE SWEEP’S OWN FILTER.** s1599 shaped its candidate set by commit-MESSAGE PREFIX (`fix|feat|perf|test|refactor`). **Two of my three genuine misses land under subjects that name something else entirely:** `c8807b4d` ships a whole new player-facing panel under *“bench: prime-agent heat 1 closes DNF×3 (operability trilogy)”*, and `7e5fae14` is a bare *“Merge branch ‘lane/c’ into HEAD”*. **A message-prefix filter cannot see either, and no amount of widening the sink fixes that** — it is the same law the house already wrote after w1-07 (never judge shipped-ness by commit messages), reappearing in the instrument that judges newsworthiness. ➡️ **Shape the candidate set by TOUCHED PATHS, never by subject.** 📐 **The trestle miss also has a sibling control, which is what raised it from “arguably” to “owed”:** `beauty-e2-pressure-garden` (`25890bae…`) and `beauty-e2-incline` (`fa5ed7ca…`) both carry gazette items; the trestle pass is the same class of work by the same hands and had none. Two of three announced is not a judgement call, it is a gap.

